questions.prompt:
Consider this C# code:

```csharp
class Meter
{
    public int Reading;
}

static void Reset(Meter item)
{
    item.Reading = 12;
    item = new Meter { Reading = 0 };
}

var current = new Meter { Reading = 7 };
var saved = current;
Reset(current);
```

Explain why `current.Reading` and `saved.Reading` are both `12` afterward. Then explain what each would contain if the parameter were changed to `ref Meter item` and the call to `Reset(ref current)`. Distinguish the caller's variables from the objects they reference.

A developer writes these methods:

```csharp
static bool TryGetLimit(bool available, out int limit)
{
    if (!available)
        return false;

    limit = 30;
    return true;
}

static int ReadLimit(in int limit)
{
    limit = 30;
    return limit;
}
```

Neither method compiles. Explain each error in terms of access to caller storage. For `TryGetLimit`, explain why returning `false` does not remove the assignment requirement and give a minimal correction. Also explain why an uninitialized local can be passed as `out` but cannot be passed as `in`.

questions.model:
`out limit` aliases the caller's variable and must be assigned before every normal return. Returning `false` is still a normal return; the Boolean does not change that contract. A minimal correction is:

```csharp
if (!available)
{
    limit = 0;
    return false;
}
```

That assignment writes `0` into the caller's variable even though the method reports failure.

`in limit` permits reading through the parameter but forbids assigning to it. Removing `limit = 30;` makes `ReadLimit` return the supplied value.

An `out` argument can be an uninitialized local because the method must assign it before returning normally. An `in` argument must already be initialized because the method receives an existing value to read.
